var e={kindLabels:{"press-release":`Press Release`,"founder-note":`Founder Note`,"product-launch":`Product Launch`},posts:[{slug:`making-complex-products-understandable`,ogImage:`/og/news-making-complex-products-understandable.png`,ogEyebrow:`Why SolCrys Exists`,ogTitleParts:[`Making complex products`,`understandable.`],ogSubtitle:`Eason Wang on the problem SolCrys exists to solve, why it is urgent now, and the Loop we use to solve it.`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Making Complex Products Understandable`,metaTitle:`Making Complex Products Understandable: Why SolCrys Exists`,description:`Eason Wang on why SolCrys exists: to make complex products understandable to the people who need them, why answer engines make it urgent, and the Loop.`,dek:`Why SolCrys exists, why the problem is urgent now, and the method we use to solve it.`,date:`2026-10-29`,datePublished:`2026-10-29`,updated:`2026-10-30`,tag:`Vision`,author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},relatedSlugs:[`a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold`,`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`],body:[{type:`heading`,text:`The problem we exist to solve`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The best product in a category does not always win. Often the winner is the product buyers understand.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That gap is widest for complex products: a new processor architecture, a network fabric, an energy-management platform, a developer API that replaces a team. The people who build them know exactly why they matter. The people who buy them have to piece that together from fragments, on a deadline, often before they ever talk to the builder.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`More and more often, the first explanation a buyer hears comes from an AI answer engine. Someone asks ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overviews, Perplexity, or Claude what a product does, how it compares, and whether it fits, and the answer becomes their first working model of the product. If that answer is missing, vague, outdated, or wrong, the product can lose the buyer before anyone on its team knew the buyer existed.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**SolCrys exists to make complex products understandable to the people who need them.** Today we start where more and more buyers start, with answer engines. Our discipline is Answer Engine Optimization, and our method is the Loop. We measure how answer engines explain a product, diagnose where the explanation breaks, execute fixes grounded in the company's own approved facts, and verify that the explanation changed. Then we run it again.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why complex products get misunderstood`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Complex products are rarely misunderstood because their makers are careless. They are misunderstood because the people who know the most are badly placed to see what a newcomer is missing, and because buyers now do most of their learning without the maker in the room.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**The builder's knowledge is the first obstacle.** Economists call it the curse of knowledge. [Camerer, Loewenstein, and Weber (1989)](https://www.cmu.edu/dietrich/sds/docs/loewenstein/CurseknowledgeEconSet.pdf) found that "better-informed agents are unable to ignore private information even when it is in their interest to do so." In their introduction, they name the case that matters here: "sellers are better informed about the true value of their products than buyers."`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`In [Elizabeth Newton's 1990 Stanford study](https://gwern.net/doc/psychology/cognitive-bias/illusion-of-depth/1990-newton.pdf), people tapped out well-known songs and predicted listeners would name about half of them. Listeners named 3 of 120, or 2.5%. Every engineer who has written a product page has been the tapper.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**The buyer's situation makes it worse.** A purchase is now a committee decision made mostly at a distance:`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`Technical buyers complete 62% of their journey online before contacting a vendor, and 21% say the technical complexity of a solution is what made them reach out ([GlobalSpec, TREW Marketing, and Elektor, 2026](https://advertising.globalspec.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/SMTE-Report_GS2602_FINAL.pdf)).`,`Before first contact, 94% of buying groups had already ranked their preferred vendors, and 77% of the time they bought from that early favorite ([6sense, 2025 Buyer Experience Report](https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251112018032/en), more than 4,000 buyers).`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So a complex product has to be understood by a group of people who have usually picked a favorite before they talk to the company that built it.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**Confusion rarely ends in a no. It ends in nothing.** In a study of more than 2.5 million recorded sales conversations, [Matthew Dixon and Ted McKenna](https://hbr.org/2022/06/stop-losing-sales-to-customer-indecision) found that 40% to 60% of deals are lost to customers who intended to buy and then failed to act.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Lab research shows how little it takes. When the same two products were described in a hard-to-read font, the share of people who put off choosing rose from 17% to 41% ([Novemsky, Dhar, Schwarz, and Simonson, 2007](https://spinup-000d1a-wp-offload-media.s3.amazonaws.com/faculty/wp-content/uploads/sites/48/2019/06/PreferenceFluencyinChoice.pdf)). In a 650-person experiment, technical jargon led to "increased risk perceptions, and lower support for technology adoption" ([Bullock and colleagues, 2019](https://doi.org/10.1177/0963662519865687)).`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The pattern is consistent. What a buyer cannot understand feels risky, and what feels risky gets postponed. For a complex product, understanding is not a communications nicety: it is the step between being found and being chosen, and it is where much of the value leaks out.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why now: the explainer changed`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`For 25 years, a company's website was the authoritative explanation of its product, and search engines sent buyers to it. Now the explanation often happens inside the answer, before the buyer visits anything.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**The habit formed in about two years.** Across 6 countries, the share of people who used generative AI to get information in the past week rose from 11% in 2024 to 24% in 2025 and 38% in 2026 ([Reuters Institute, October 2026](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/generative-ai-and-news-report-2026-standalone-tools-information-infrastructure)). Google says AI Overviews has more than [2.5 billion monthly users](https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/sundar-pichai-io-2026/), and the Gemini app has [reached 1 billion users](https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001652044/000165204426000080/googexhibit991q32026.htm). OpenAI says ChatGPT has [1.2 billion weekly users](https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/29/openais-latest-features-take-direct-aim-at-the-app-store-model/).`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**The answer stands in for the visit, most of all on hard questions.** In [Pew Research Center's study](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/07/22/google-users-are-less-likely-to-click-on-links-when-an-ai-summary-appears-in-the-results/) of 68,879 searches, people clicked a result on 8% of visits when an AI summary appeared and on 15% when none did. They clicked a link inside the summary on 1% of visits. Long questions drew summaries far more often: 53% of searches of 10 words or more, against 8% of one- or two-word searches.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The questions a complex product raises are long ones.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**Buyers rely on the answers, and people tend to trust them.** In [G2's March 2026 survey](https://company.g2.com/news/g2-research-the-answer-economy) of 1,076 B2B software buyers and decision-makers, half (51%) said they now start their research with an AI chatbot more often than with Google. Across 9 countries, 75% of people who used AI to answer factual questions said they trust the answers ([Reuters Institute, October 2026](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/generative-ai-and-news-report-2026-standalone-tools-information-infrastructure)). When an answer is wrong, that trust carries the mistake into the buyer's first working model of the product.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**New and specialized products are the ones AI explains worst.** Language models are weakest on facts that appear rarely in what they learned from. Researchers found that models "struggle with less popular factual knowledge, and that scaling fails to appreciably improve memorization" of it ([Mallen and colleagues, ACL 2023](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.10511)). Researchers at OpenAI and Georgia Tech [showed](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04664) that for arbitrary facts with no pattern to learn, a base model will hallucinate at least as often as the share of those facts it saw only once in training.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Google's own account of AI Overviews errors points to ["data voids,"](https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/search/ai-overviews-update-may-2024/) topics with "a limited amount of high quality content." A new processor architecture or a category term coined last quarter is, almost by definition, a thinly documented fact.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**The answer is becoming the place where people act.** Google says its new checkout protocol already enables direct checkout for ["hundreds of thousands of brands and retailers"](https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/products/shopping/google-shopping-updates-holiday-shopping/) across Google. In the same announcement, it reported that product attributes supplied by lululemon were used in 50% of relevant AI recommendations during testing: what a company tells the system shapes what the buyer is told.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**We have seen a shift like this before.** In 1998, [Sergey Brin and Larry Page](https://snap.stanford.edu/class/cs224w-readings/Brin98Anatomy.pdf) argued that hand-built web directories "cannot cover all esoteric topics," and search replaced them; by 2008, Google was publishing its own [Search Engine Optimization Starter Guide](https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2008/11/googles-seo-starter-guide). A discipline grew up around being found. The new front door needs one built around being explained correctly: for a simple product, being found is most of the work, but for a complex one, the explanation is the work.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The world we are building toward`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`[Last month I wrote](/news/a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold/) that an AI-ready brand is "found, understood accurately, and chosen, by the engines and by the buyers they send." Understanding is the middle step: an engine has to find a product before it can explain it, and a buyer has to understand a product before choosing it. That middle step is the hardest to earn and the most valuable to own, and it is what SolCrys is built around.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`When we succeed, four things will be true.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Any answer engine can explain a complex product accurately, at the depth the asker needs.** A CFO, an architect, and an engineer ask different questions about the same product. Each gets an answer that is correct, current, and in terms they can use, and each answer traces back to facts the company stands behind.`,`**Companies govern how they are explained.** A company already governs its financial statements, its legal claims, and its brand guidelines, but not yet the explanation most buyers meet first. We expect that to change: a company's approved facts, claims, and limits become infrastructure, maintained like a codebase and used by every surface that explains the product.`,`**The buyer's question becomes the unit of marketing work.** Not the campaign and not the keyword, but the question a buyer brings, and whether the answer to it is right. Progress is measured as a verified change in how the product is explained.`,`**Buyers decide with less fear.** If understanding is the step between found and chosen, better explanations shorten the stall. Fewer good products lose to "no decision" because a buyer could not explain them to their own committee.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer engines are where we start, for two reasons. They are the newest explainer and the least governed one, and they are the one place where an explanation can be measured the same way, again and again, at scale. The discipline does not stop there: the same facts should explain the product on the company's website, at its booth, and in its demos, and to the software agents that will increasingly research and buy on a person's behalf.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The method: the Loop`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Our method is a closed loop of four steps, Measure, Diagnose, Execute, and Verify, run again and again against the same buyer questions and the same approved facts.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Why a loop and not a campaign? Control engineers put the principle in one line: ["base correcting actions on the difference between desired and actual performance"](https://introcontrol.mit.edu/_static/fall21/extras/Feedback%20Systems%20Murray.pdf) (Åström and Murray, Feedback Systems). A campaign is open loop: it publishes and moves on, without checking whether the explanation changed.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That fails for answer engines, because the explanation keeps moving. Models are updated, competitors publish, and sources age, so a fix that is never checked decays without anyone noticing. A closed loop is how an explanation stays correct.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Measure.** Build a set of buyer questions around who is asking, what they need, and how far along they are. Run them on ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overviews, Perplexity, and Claude, and record how each engine explains the product today.`,`**Diagnose.** Find where the explanation breaks: the product is absent, the right sources are not cited, the facts are wrong or out of date, or a competitor's framing wins. Answer Accuracy grades answers against the company's approved facts, and Workspace Deep Analysis looks across every question to find the root causes behind many symptoms.`,`**Execute.** Turn the diagnosis into work grounded in Corporate Context and routed for human review: a clearer category page, a missing comparison, a corrected fact, a demo that shows what a paragraph cannot. The company's team publishes the work, or SolCrys builds it for them.`,`**Verify.** Rerun the same questions against the baseline, judge the change across several runs, and feed what changed into the next round.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Two details make this a method rather than a slogan. First, the questions and the facts hold still within a turn, so before and after are compared on equal terms. Between turns, the facts grow as Diagnose surfaces what the company never wrote down.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Second, Verify is deliberately patient. The same textbook warns that feedback brings in "sensor noise" that needs "careful filtering," and answer engines are noisy sensors: the same question can get different answers on different runs. Judging across runs is how we avoid chasing noise.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`W. Edwards Deming insisted that his improvement cycle be called [Plan, Do, Study, Act](https://businesswales.gov.wales/sites/main/files/documents/Foundation%20and%20the%20history%20of%20the%20PDSA%20cycle.pdf), not Check. The spirit carries over: each turn is something we study and learn from, not a box we tick.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Cornelis Networks shows what this looks like with a deeply technical product. It needed buyers and engines to understand a brand-new networking category, so we told one story at three depths, from the homepage to the category page to a reference architecture, and measured each piece. "SolCrys AI took a deeply technical concept and turned it into real content mapped to different technical levels, and measured the effectiveness of every piece they produced," said Brandon Draeger, chief marketing officer of Cornelis Networks. At the company's trade show booth, an interactive demo turned the reference architecture into "something a visitor could grasp in two minutes," said Nishant Lodha, senior director of marketing ([case study](/customers/cornelis/)).`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why the work compounds`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Loop is a discipline, not a moat. Anyone can draw four verbs on a slide. What compounds is what each turn of the Loop leaves behind.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**A governed source of truth.** Corporate Context holds a company's approved facts, claims, and guardrails, set once for the whole organization. Each turn adds to it or corrects it, because a wrong answer found in Diagnose often exposes a fact the company never wrote down. Over time it becomes the company's most complete, current description of its product, and the one source for every output, whether the first reader is a person or a model.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**Comparable history.** Every action is tied to the same buyer questions, measured the same way before and after, so the record shows what changed after each piece of work, on which engine, for which kind of question. A company that has run the Loop for a year knows how its category gets explained in a way a newcomer cannot buy. Across many categories, we learn which kinds of work tend to move answers: patterns, never one customer's private facts.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**A harness that outlives any model.** The models we use will change many times. What carries over is the harness around them: the scenarios we test against, the labeled examples that define a good answer, and the record of how each model performed on our jobs. I made that argument at length in [Models Change. The Harness Compounds.](/models-change-harness-compounds/)`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**Trust that is slow to build.** Companies with complex or regulated products will not let an outside system say things about them without review. Approval, and a record of where each claim came from, have to be designed in from the start. They are hard to bolt on later, and they are what lets a cautious company say yes.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`**A path from diagnosis to the finished fix.** Many teams can see the gap but lack the people to close it. When a fix needs a new category page, an interactive demo, or a booth animation, [SolCrys Sites](/sites/) and [SolCrys Motion](/motion/) build it from the same Corporate Context, so the explanation stays consistent wherever the buyer meets it.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`For a customer, these assets grow more valuable with every turn: a longer verified history and a fuller set of governed facts. For SolCrys, each customer and each category make the method better for the next one.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What has to be true`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`A vision is a set of bets. These are ours, with the evidence we have today and the signal that would tell us we are wrong.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Answer engines stay a primary explainer for considered purchases.** Evidence today: Weekly AI use for information rose to 38% in 2026, and half of B2B software buyers now start their research with an AI chatbot more often than with Google. We are wrong if buyers of complex products move their research back to vendor sites, analysts, and people.`,`**What a company publishes still shapes how it is explained.** Evidence today: Google's account of AI Overviews errors points to "data voids"; brand-supplied attributes were used in 50% of relevant recommendations in Google's test; and our customers' category questions moved after they published. We are wrong if engines lean so heavily on third-party, community, or paid sources that a company's own facts stop mattering.`,`**Companies pay for verified change, not for dashboards.** Evidence today: Basic tracking is already offered free by many tools, so measurement alone is not where the value sits. We are wrong if teams buy cheap tracking and do the rest by hand, without losing ground.`,`**Accuracy and governance beat raw speed.** Evidence today: Most people who use AI for facts trust the answers, so a wrong answer misleads before anyone catches it, and technical and regulated companies require review. We are wrong if engines reward volume over accuracy, and fast, ungoverned content wins for long enough to matter.`,`**Understanding can be measured, not just asserted.** Evidence today: We can grade how engines explain a product against approved facts. We are wrong if we cannot connect better explanations to better buyer decisions.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The last bet is the one we have proven least, so I want to be plain about it. Today we measure how answer engines explain a product: whether it is mentioned, which sources are cited, and whether the claims match the company's approved facts. We do not yet measure what a buyer understands after reading the answer, and building that measure is part of the work ahead.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The early results point the right way. Working with Cornelis Networks, we helped make a brand-new networking category answerable in four weeks: the mention rate on that category's questions rose 12-fold, on a term nobody had published before, and the company's overall AI mention rate rose nearly 7 percentage points ([case study](/customers/cornelis/)). NextSilicon's AI mention rate on HPC and AI questions rose from 1.9% to 7.4% in 45 days ([case study](/customers/nextsilicon/)).`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`These are early results from two categories. As the evidence grows, we will publish it with its sample sizes, by the standard I set last month: a lift shown without a sample size is a picture, not a finding.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Questions we hear`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Isn't this SEO under a new name?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SEO earned its place by helping companies get found. For a complex product, the harder problem comes next: being explained correctly, and that now happens inside the answer. The work overlaps with good SEO, such as clear, well-structured pages, but the unit is a buyer's question and the measure is the answer itself.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Won't the answer engines fix this on their own, or take facts straight from brands?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`An engine can only explain what is written down somewhere it trusts, and the facts about a new product have to come from the company that makes it. As engines build ways to take those facts in directly, the question shifts from whether a company supplies facts to which facts it supplies, and whether they are approved, current, and consistent. That makes a governed source of truth more valuable, not less.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`How large is this?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Every company with a complex product already pays to be explained: its website, product marketing, content, events, analyst relations, and sales engineers. What is new is that the most influential explainer is a system none of those budgets governs. We expect governing it to become a standing part of how those companies spend.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Why SolCrys?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We built for the hardest case first. Our deepest work is with products that are hard to explain, in high-performance computing and AI infrastructure, where a vague answer costs the most. Governance was there from the start: Corporate Context holds each customer's approved facts, and Answer Accuracy grades engine answers against them. Many tools now track whether engines mention a brand; our bet is on what comes after tracking: governed facts, finished fixes, and verified change.`},{type:`heading`,text:`How we work, and what we refuse`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`A vision only matters if it changes what we do on an ordinary Monday. Alignment is rarer than it looks: in [a study of 124 companies](https://sloanreview.mit.edu/article/no-one-knows-your-strategy-not-even-your-top-leaders/), only 28% of executives and middle managers responsible for executing strategy could list three of their company's strategic priorities. Ours fit in one line: make complex products understandable, run the Loop honestly, and let what compounds compound.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`These principles are how we decide.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Accuracy before reach.** We never make a product sound better than it is. A buyer who understands a product correctly and walks away is a better outcome than one who buys on a misunderstanding.`,`**Start from the buyer's question.** We work backward from what a buyer needs to understand at their stage, as [Amazon's principle](https://www.amazon.jobs/content/en/our-workplace/leadership-principles) puts it: "start with the customer and work backwards."`,`**Claim only what we can verify.** Same questions, same baseline, judged across runs, with the method and the sample size stated.`,`**Facts are governed, and people approve.** Anything we draft for a customer is grounded in their Corporate Context and goes to their team for review before it ships.`,`**Choose models by evidence, not reputation.** We compare models on our own scenarios and labeled examples, and let the results guide the choice.`,`**The process is not the thing.** Jeff Bezos warned that a process can become ["the proxy for the result you want."](https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/2016-letter-to-shareholders) Running the Loop is not the goal. A buyer who understands the product is.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`And these are the things we will not do, even when they would move a number:`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`Manipulate answer engines with hidden text, planted instructions, fake reviews, or coordinated posting. Researchers have shown that a ["strategic text sequence"](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07981) can push a product up an AI's recommendations, and that is exactly the kind of win we refuse.`,`Invent or inflate a claim, a rating, or a number, for a customer or for ourselves.`,`Publish anything under a customer's name without their review.`,`Call a single run's movement a result before we have rerun it and judged it across runs.`,`Attack competitors with claims we cannot source.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We will debate these bets hard. Once we decide, we [disagree and commit](https://www.aboutamazon.com/news/company-news/2016-letter-to-shareholders). Whatever their role, everyone at SolCrys should be able to name the step of the Loop their work serves and the evidence that will show it worked.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The goal is simple to state. When a buyer asks an answer engine about a complex product, the answer should be one its makers would sign.`}]},{slug:`introducing-workspace-deep-analysis`,ogImage:`/og/news-introducing-workspace-deep-analysis.png`,ogTitleParts:[`Workspace Deep Analysis.`,`One plan for the next four weeks.`],ogSubtitle:`Run one analysis a month. Get back a short, ranked, dated plan, with every action grounded in the latest AI answers and citations.`,kind:`product-launch`,title:`SolCrys AEO Workspace Deep Analysis: one plan for the next four weeks`,metaTitle:`SolCrys AEO Workspace Deep Analysis: one plan for the next four weeks`,description:`Today we're launching Workspace Deep Analysis. Run one analysis a month across the prompts that matter most to you and get back a short, ranked, dated plan, with every action grounded in the latest AI answers and citations.`,date:`2026-10-13`,datePublished:`2026-10-13`,updated:`2026-10-13`,tag:`Launch`,relatedSlugs:[`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`,`your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Our best product ideas come straight from customers. Ours have been telling us three things: they want evidence, not just insights. They want those insights to fit the way their team already works. And they want an easier way to implement them.`},{type:`quote`,text:`I don't have time to check in every day. Is there an easier way to come in and know what I need to do in the next month?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So that's what we built: evidence behind every finding, insights that land in the day to day, and the shortest path from finding to done.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today we're launching Workspace Deep Analysis. Run one analysis a month across the prompts that matter most to you. Get back a short, ranked, dated plan that covers your month, with every action grounded in the latest AI-generated answers and citations, one click from Actions Hub.`},{type:`heading`,text:`From one prompt to the whole workspace`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Deep Analysis started at the prompt level. Pick one question, and SolCrys reads every AI answer and every page those answers cite to explain why the engines say what they say about you.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Customers loved the why. Then they asked for the natural next step: across all of this, what should my team do first, and how do we get it done?`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`And there are several ways to think about which prompts to prioritize. Is this the most important prompt you're working on right now, the one you're writing content for, or pitching third-party editorials to cover? Is your goal to lift long-tail prompts, or to build on the ones where you already perform well? Or to improve one particular engine? All great questions to think through. But when you have 10 meetings a day, you probably don't have much time to think through them every week.`},{type:`timeline`,caption:`Ten launches in six months, all driven by real customer needs, from SolCrys AEO in May 2026 to local business and person AEO in September–October 2026.`,entries:[{date:`May 2026`,title:`SolCrys AEO`,quote:`What is AI saying about us?`},{date:`Jun 2026`,title:`MCP`,quote:`Can we use this without leaving our tools?`},{date:`Jul 2026`,title:`Answer Accuracy`,quote:`Is what AI says about us actually right?`},{date:`Jul 2026`,title:`Recommendation`,quote:`Does being mentioned mean being chosen?`},{date:`Jul 2026`,title:`Corporate Context Engine`,quote:`How do we keep AI current as our business changes?`},{date:`Jul 2026`,title:`Community Intelligence`,quote:`Where are the conversations that shape AI answers?`},{date:`Aug 2026`,title:`Signals (Market Watch)`,quote:`What is changing in our market this week?`},{date:`Sep 2026`,title:`Web and animation`,quote:`Can the same facts power our website and our booth?`},{date:`Sep–Oct 2026`,title:`Local business, person AEO`,quote:`Can AEO track our CEO and CTO?`}]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Workspace Deep Analysis is built for exactly that step. Not another report to interpret, but a plan to work through.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What one analysis gives you`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Pick up to 10 prompts that ask about the same need, such as setup, pricing, or comparisons, across your AI engines. Start the analysis and have a coffee or tea; it reads the answers and the cited pages and usually finishes in 5 to 15 minutes.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The report opens on Diagnosis:`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**A one-sentence verdict** that sums up where you stand, before any detail.`,`**What's working.** The sources that already help you, like the roundup article every engine cites or the page that names your brand. Keep them accurate and current.`,`**What holds you back.** Each finding is typed as a content gap, competitor advantage, or owned content not cited, and comes with its evidence: the engine, the answer's own words, the prompt, and the page the answer cited.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`No guessing why AI leaves you out. For every issue, you see which AI assistant said it, the question that was asked, the exact words of the answer, and the website it sent readers to instead of yours.`},{type:`heading`,text:`A dated four-week plan, one click from the work`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Actions tab is the part customers asked for most. It sums up the plan in one sentence, then lists Week 1: the tasks most likely to make a difference. Weeks 2–3 hold the rest, grouped by where you publish: your own site, other sites' articles and listings, and social or video platforms.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Actions fit to your business domain, your AI visibility gaps, and your Corporate Context. Every card tells you where to publish, which answer gap it closes, the key points to cover, and its effort and impact. Select **Add to Actions** on the tasks you plan to do, and the work lands in Actions Hub with a brief attached, ready for an owner, a due date, and a path from Todo to Published.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Why four weeks? Week 1 starts the day the report finishes, and Weeks 2–3 cover the 14 days after. The final week leaves room for your changes to show up in AI answers before you measure again next month.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Measure visibility. Diagnose gaps. Execute the right actions. Verify what moved. Workspace Deep Analysis runs the whole loop on a monthly rhythm: run one analysis, work the plan for four weeks, then compare mentions and recommendation scores on the same prompts and run the next one. The results come from doing the work, not from running the analysis again.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Three examples`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Say you run a well-established business in San Francisco and you want to expand to San Jose. When someone in San Jose asks an AI assistant who to call, the answers probably name the businesses already there, not you.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Pick up to 10 prompts your future customers would ask, like "best [your service] in San Jose" or "who should I call for [your service] in San Jose", and run one analysis. The report shows which AI assistants skip you, what they say instead, and the pages they trust: local roundups, review sites, a competitor's comparison page. The plan that comes back is your first month of expansion marketing. Week 1 might be a San Jose page on your own site; Weeks 2 and 3, the listings and roundups the answers already cite. Add the tasks to Actions Hub, assign owners, ship. Next month, run it again and see how often the answers name you for San Jose.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Or say you're writing the launch plan for a new feature. Run an analysis before launch on the prompts the feature should win: "best tools for [the job it does]", "[your category] with [the capability]", "[your product] vs [competitor]". The report shows what the answers say today and which pages they pull from, and the plan becomes the AEO section of your launch checklist: the pages to publish on launch day in Week 1, the comparison and editorial coverage to pitch in Weeks 2 and 3. A month later, run it again and see whether the answers mention the new feature when it matters.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It works for people, too. Say you want to track your CEO's visibility. Run an analysis on the prompts where executives get named, like "top voices in [your industry]" or "[topic] experts to follow". The report shows which AI assistants mention your CEO, what they say, and the interviews, podcasts, and articles they pull from, and the plan tells you which of those to pitch next.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Every action carries its evidence`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Customers told us they only trust an action when the evidence convinces them it's worth doing. We built the whole report around that idea.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Every task in a Workspace Deep Analysis plan links back to the answer gaps it closes, and every gap shows the AI answers behind it, with the cited page. If a destination can't be confirmed, the card says so instead of pretending.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`And the plan runs through quality checks before it reaches you. An action the data can't back gets dropped. Some months the report will say "no actions this time" and explain why. We'd rather hand you a shorter plan than a padded one.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Impact is an estimate tied to named engines and prompts, not a promise. You choose what goes to Actions Hub, your team approves what ships, and next month's measurement shows what changed. Humans stay in control of every step.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Available today, on every plan`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Workspace Deep Analysis is live today on every SolCrys plan; each run uses one Deep Analysis from your monthly allowance. The [user guide](/guides/workspace-deep-analysis/) has more details.`}]},{slug:`a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold`,ogImage:`/og/news-a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold.png`,ogEyebrow:`A Year in View`,ogTitleParts:[`The models kept changing.`,`The facts had to hold.`],ogSubtitle:`Eason Wang on what changed in AI answers, models, and our product in year one, and 5 predictions for year two.`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`A Year in View: The Models Kept Changing. The Facts Had to Hold.`,metaTitle:`A Year in View: The Models Kept Changing. The Facts Had to Hold.`,description:`Eason Wang on year one of answer engine optimization: how buyers, engines, and models changed, what we built and deleted, and 5 predictions for year two.`,date:`2026-09-26`,datePublished:`2026-09-26`,updated:`2026-09-26`,tag:`Year One`,author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},relatedSlugs:[`your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys`,`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Gwen wrote about [why we started and whom we listened to](/news/your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys/). This note is the other half of year one: what changed underneath us. Three things moved at once over the past 12 months. Buyers moved into AI answers. The models we build on became cheaper and more capable nearly every month. And our product changed shape several times in response, sometimes by adding things and sometimes by deleting them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If I had to compress the year into one sentence, it would be this: generation got cheap and models became replaceable parts, so the scarce inputs became the facts a brand can stand behind and the evidence that something actually worked. What follows is how we got there, organized around the questions customers asked us, in their words, roughly in the order they asked them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`1. “What is AI saying about us?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`When we started in September 2025, buyers were already ahead of the brands they were evaluating. In Forrester's [State of Business Buying 2026](https://www.forrester.com/blogs/state-of-business-buying-2026/), 94% of business buyers said they use AI somewhere in their buying process. [G2 found](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-g2-research-half-of-b2b-software-buyers-now-start-their-research-with-ai-chatbots-302742807.html) that 51% of B2B software buyers now start their research in an AI chatbot more often than in Google, up from 29% in April 2025. At the same time, the clicks that used to tell marketers what was happening started falling away. Chartbeat data in the [Reuters Institute's 2026 trends report](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/journalism-media-and-technology-trends-and-predictions-2026) shows Google organic referrals to news sites falling 33% worldwide and 38% in the U.S. in the year to November 2025.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So the first question was simple, and almost nobody could answer it: what does AI say about us? The technology to answer it arrived just in time. On September 29, 2025, Anthropic released Claude Sonnet 4.5, renamed its Claude Code SDK the Claude Agent SDK, and published an engineering essay that helped give the year its vocabulary: [context engineering](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents), the idea that what a model reads matters as much as how you ask it. Search-grounded model APIs made it practical to put a buyer's question to an engine, over and over, and record what came back.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Two lessons from those months still shape the product. The first is that answers are not stable. Ask the same question twice and you get a different list. [SparkToro's study](https://sparktoro.com/blog/new-research-ais-are-highly-inconsistent-when-recommending-brands-or-products-marketers-should-take-care-when-tracking-ai-visibility/) of 2,961 runs later put a number on it: you would wait more than 1,000 runs to see two brand lists come back in the same order. The same study concluded that visibility measured as a rate across many runs is a reasonable metric, and that a "ranking position in AI" is not. We had reached the same conclusion from our own data, which is why our core metrics are rates across repeated runs, not the rank in a single answer.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The second is that people want to use this where they already work. We built our first MCP server in October 2025, before we had an AEO product and 2 months before MCP moved into the Linux Foundation's new [Agentic AI Foundation](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-12-09-mcp-joins-agentic-ai-foundation/). By July 2026, the protocol's main SDKs were [approaching half a billion downloads a month](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/). MCP is now [how customers bring SolCrys into Claude, ChatGPT, and Cursor](/why-we-bet-on-mcp/), and it can act, not just read: since June, a customer's own assistant can mark an Action Hub task as published, through a separate permission they opt into.`},{type:`heading`,text:`2. “What do we do about it?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`By early 2026 the category had a name. In March, Gartner published its first [Market Guide for Answer Engine Visibility Tools](https://www.gartner.com/en/documents/7559273). The name was accurate about where the market stood: the tools were built to see. In May, [Google's own guidance](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide) gave Google's answer to a debate that had run all year: "optimizing for generative AI search is optimizing for the search experience, and thus still SEO." For Google's AI surfaces, answer engine optimization is an operating layer on top of SEO foundations, not a replacement for them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Seeing a gap is not the same as closing it, and closing it became the next question. The model side moved fastest here. In February, Claude Opus 4.6 arrived with a million-token context window in beta, and OpenAI described an internal project in which a small team [shipped about a million lines of code in 5 months without writing any of it by hand](https://openai.com/index/harness-engineering/). They called the discipline harness engineering. In April, Anthropic put the lesson in one sentence: "[Harnesses encode assumptions that go stale as models improve](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/managed-agents)." The model does the work. The harness decides what it reads, what it is allowed to do, and how its work gets checked, and it has to be rebuilt as the models change.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is the shape we gave the product. In May we launched SolCrys AEO as a loop, Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify, instead of a dashboard. Measure and Diagnose were the parts we already knew how to build. Execute had to be governed: drafts grounded in a company's approved facts, and a person approving anything before it ships. Verify turned out to be the hardest verb in the loop.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Why we deleted our first Verify`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Our first verification system re-checked published pages and reported impact at 7, 14, and 30 days. The numbers looked clean. But it had no control prompts and no confidence intervals, and our internal review in May concluded that we could not defend the headline numbers. We removed it that month. What replaced it is deliberately narrower: rerun the same frozen prompt set on the same engines, re-audit the same page to see whether its score recovered, and judge a change by whether it holds across runs, not by a single before-and-after number.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`This matters beyond our product. In July, a [survey of 45 GEO studies](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14035) concluded that "no reviewed technique shows a stable, longitudinal, cross-platform causal effect." That doesn't mean nothing works. It means the burden of proof sits with whoever claims a result, including us. A lift shown without a sample size is a picture, not a finding.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`The harness we use on ourselves`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We build SolCrys the way we think marketing will increasingly run: agents do much of the work inside a harness that people design. We merged about 1,900 pull requests in our first year, and in August and September close to 9 in 10 were marked as written with a coding agent. The human work moved to specs, evaluations, and review, with a second model from a different family often reviewing alongside. The discipline we sell is the one we apply to our own code: approved context, governed execution, and honest verification.`},{type:`heading`,text:`3. “Is it right, and does it choose us?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Once teams could see how they appeared, they asked harder questions: is what AI says about us true, and does being mentioned mean being chosen? The honest answer to both: not reliably. [The same G2 survey](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-g2-research-half-of-b2b-software-buyers-now-start-their-research-with-ai-chatbots-302742807.html) found that 64% of software buyers get inaccurate recommendations from AI chatbots often or very often. [A study of Google AI Overviews](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14021) by Xu, Iqbal, and Montgomery found that 11.0% of 98,020 claims were not supported by the pages cited for them. And [Lily Ray's analysis](https://searchengineland.com/google-ai-overviews-cite-self-serving-listicles-recommend-competitors-480573) of B2B software queries found that when AI Overviews cited a brand's own "best of" listicle, 224 of 323 times the brand itself was left out of the recommendation. Being a source and being the answer are different things.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Accuracy also started to look like a legal question. In May, the [Regional Court of Munich](https://the-decoder.com/landmark-german-ruling-declares-googles-ai-overviews-are-googles-own-words-and-makes-it-liable-for-false-answers/) held that Google's AI Overviews are Google's own content, not a list of third-party results, and that Google is liable for false statements in them. The ruling is not final, and Google has said it is reviewing it. The direction is still worth noticing: what an engine says about your company is starting to count as something someone said.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The technology change that let us answer these questions was context length. In January our knowledge pipeline still worked the old way: embed documents, retrieve the closest fragments, and hope the right fragment came back. By June, context windows were large and cheap enough that we stopped retrieving fragments of a company's truth. Answer Accuracy grades each AI answer against the company's entire Corporate Context, and it cannot mark a claim wrong without pointing to the evidence. [Recommendation Score](/ai-recommendation-score/) measures how favorably an engine endorses a brand against its competitors, and every point traces back to quoted text in the answer.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is why the [Corporate Context Engine](/news/introducing-the-corporate-context-engine/) sits underneath everything we shipped after it. Engineers spent the year learning that the model matters less than what it reads. For a brand, what the model reads is its approved facts, claims, and proof. Keep those current and consistent, and every output built on them, from an AI answer to a webpage to a booth, inherits the accuracy. Let them drift, and no model upgrade will fix it.`},{type:`heading`,text:`4. What holds when the ground moves`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`August put that to the test. In the second week of the month, ChatGPT all but stopped citing Reddit. Independent trackers, [as reported by Search Engine Journal](https://www.searchenginejournal.com/why-reddits-chatgpt-citation-drop-isnt-fully-explained/586479/), measured Reddit's share of ChatGPT citations falling from 3.83% to 0.52% within about a week. In our own tracking across 5 engines, the break was ChatGPT's alone: Google AI Overviews, Perplexity, and Gemini barely moved, and the citations ChatGPT dropped were replaced mostly by vendors' official sites, documentation, and government pages.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It had happened before. The same report notes that in September 2025, several trackers saw Reddit's share of ChatGPT citations collapse within a few weeks. Two drops in a year, on one engine, with no change in what Reddit users were writing, tell you what kind of variable this is. Which sources an engine names is decided at least as much by licensing, retrieval policy, and cost as by the quality of the content. We changed our own stance to match: we now treat Reddit as a place engines read to learn what customers think, rather than a citation to be won.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It also showed why measurement has to follow the surface buyers use. In our own side-by-side runs, ChatGPT's developer API and the ChatGPT app did not cite the same sources, so a team measuring only through the API sees a different citation picture from the one its buyers see. We moved more of our own measurement closer to the answers buyers actually see, with each prompt sent the way a person would type it.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Agentic commerce had a similar year. OpenAI launched Instant Checkout in ChatGPT in September 2025 and [phased it out in March 2026](https://www.digitalcommerce360.com/2026/03/24/openai-agentic-commerce-updates-chatgpt-walmart/), after [Walmart reported](https://searchengineland.com/walmart-chatgpt-checkout-converted-worse-472071) that purchases made inside ChatGPT converted at one-third the rate of click-outs to its own site. OpenAI [shut down its Atlas browser](https://9to5mac.com/2026/08/04/openai-explains-what-will-happen-when-chatgpt-atlas-shuts-down-this-weekend/) on August 9. And only 9% of B2B software buyers told [G2](https://company.g2.com/news/buyer-behavior-2026) they were comfortable letting an agent execute purchases, even within approved guardrails. For now, discovery increasingly happens in AI, and most decisions still close on the brand's own surface. That is a large part of why we started building webpages with customers like [Cornelis](/customers/cornelis/): the page an engine cites and the page a buyer lands on should tell the same story, in the same words.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The models moved just as fast. Anthropic alone shipped Claude Opus 5, Fable 5.1, and Opus 5.5 between late July and late September, and the price of its Opus tier fell from $15 per million input tokens for Opus 4.1 a year earlier to [$4 for Opus 5.5](https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5) this week. Cheaper models are good news. But for a measurement product, every model change is a migration. When we moved to a new model generation in July, several things that had worked the day before broke. So we built the unglamorous parts: evaluation sets labeled in 2 independent blind passes with disagreements adjudicated, gates a new model has to pass before it takes over a job, and the freedom to switch providers without rewriting our code. And one rule we don't bend: we never substitute the model that queries an engine, because that would change the thing being measured.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`[Signals](/signal-weekly-market-brief/), which we added in August to brief customers on their industry and competitors, taught us a related lesson. We first filtered candidate stories with a score threshold and a confidence floor. On one 40-candidate scan, everything worth reading scored between 29 and 36 and the noise between 2 and 8, so the ordering was right, yet the threshold threw away 3 of the top 4 items. The model's self-reported confidence ran backwards: the items worth reading averaged 60, the noise 97. We now rank instead of thresholding, and confidence is a label rather than a gate. When a model's scores are ordered but not calibrated, rank them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So what holds? The layer you own: your facts, stated the same way everywhere and corroborated by sources you don't control. Citations move with policy, and models turn over every few months. A company's approved facts are the one part that should change only when someone decides to change them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What I got right, and what I got wrong`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`I published several positions on this site during the year. Here is how they held up.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`[Production is cheap, trust is scarce](/production-is-cheap-trust-is-scarce/): **held, and sharpened.** Generation kept getting cheaper while the answer itself became paid, contested ground: ChatGPT's ads business passed a [$1 billion annual run rate](https://digiday.com/media-buying/openais-chatgpt-ads-business-hits-1-billion-run-rate-as-europe-gets-self-serve-access/) in under 200 days.`,`**The same essay's figure that Wikipedia and Reddit drive more than a quarter of ChatGPT's U.S. citations: out of date.** It came from January and February 2026 data. Since August it no longer holds on ChatGPT, and the page now carries a dated correction.`,`[AI cites consensus, not authority](/ai-citations-consensus-not-authority/): **needs a correction.** Corroboration across independent sources still matters. But after August, ChatGPT leaned toward official pages and documentation, so on that engine a company's own canonical pages count for more than I gave them credit for. That page now carries a dated correction too.`,`[Most GEO advice is untestable](/testable-geo-playbook/): **held.** The variance research and the survey of 45 GEO studies point the same way: measure rates, and distrust any single before-and-after.`,`[A wrong description is worse than no mention](/how-ai-describes-your-brand/): **held, with higher stakes.** Most software buyers now say they often get inaccurate AI recommendations, and at least one court has treated an AI summary as the search engine's own statement.`,`[Agents are your website's third audience](/agent-readiness-third-audience/): **half right.** Agents already use software at scale: Honeycomb [reports](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/) that nearly 20% of its monthly interactive queries now come from agents. They are not yet buying. The protocols survived the year; ChatGPT's in-chat checkout did not.`]},{type:`heading`,text:`Five predictions for year two`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Here are 5 predictions I expect to be graded on. I'll come back to them next September.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Another source will move, and not because of quality.** At least one major engine will sharply change which sources it cites because of a licensing, retrieval, or cost decision, and independent trackers will see it within days.`,`**Visibility data will be free, and budgets will follow outcomes.** Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools already report AI impressions and citations at no cost. The paid value in AEO will move to accuracy, recommendation, and evidence that a specific action changed a specific answer.`,`**Paid and organic answers will be reported separately.** With ads running in ChatGPT and in Google's AI Overviews, marketing teams will split organic answer share from sponsored placements, the way SEO and SEM split two decades ago.`,`**Agents will read first and buy later.** Agent visits will become a line item B2B teams report for their own sites, while purchases completed entirely inside an AI chat stay a small share of B2B buying.`,`**Brand context will become infrastructure.** A governed store of approved facts, claims, and proof, with versions and approvals, will become a standard part of AEO and content platforms, and buyers will ask vendors exactly how their before-and-after numbers were measured.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`For our part, year two means more of the same discipline: measure the answers buyers actually see, engine by engine; make one Corporate Context the source for every output, whether the first reader is a person or a model; let agents take on more of the execution, with a person approving what ships; and keep verification honest.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The facts have to hold`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Gwen ended her note with a promise: your brand, AI ready. From where I sit, that means something specific. Your brand is found, understood accurately, and chosen, by the engines and by the buyers they send. The models will keep changing every few months. Our job is to make sure the facts hold.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Thank you to Gwen and Jia for a year of building together, and to every customer who told us when a number didn't look right. Those conversations made the product more rigorous.`}]},{slug:`your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys`,ogImage:`/og/news-your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Your Brand, AI Ready: One Year of SolCrys`,metaTitle:`Your Brand, AI Ready: One Year of SolCrys`,description:`SolCrys co-founder and CEO Gwen Chen on year one — why AI is now the first reader of your brand, and how one Corporate Context came to power AI answers, webpages, booth animation, and community engagement for the teams we work with.`,date:`2026-09-22`,datePublished:`2026-09-22`,updated:`2026-09-22`,tag:`Year One`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys AI`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},relatedSlugs:[`a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold`,`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`,`introducing-solcrys-aeo`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`I started my marketing career in SEO in 2007, helping a Chinese building materials startup outrank incumbents many times its size. It was my first lesson that a small team with a sharper understanding of how discovery works can beat a bigger budget. From there I moved into communications and spent five years as a journalist interviewing executives, officials, traders, and analysts, which taught me to ask better questions and to tell the difference between a claim and a fact.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Then I came to the United States, where I have now worked for ten years. I began as a growth hacker at AppSheet and branched into product marketing and GTM strategy in 2019, and along the way I did nearly everything a marketer is asked to do: SEO and SEM, case studies, running a blog site, events strategy, partner training, sales enablement, product launches, category marketing, and analyst relations.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`My scope kept changing, but the pressures never did. There was always more to do than any team could take on, so every planning cycle began with what marketing should prioritize. Leadership always wanted proof that the work mattered, so every review came back to how we measure success. Every launch set the bar for the next one, so every retrospective asked how we get better with each cycle. And behind all of it sat the question that matters most, which is how we serve customers well.`},{type:`heading`,text:`AI changed both sides of marketing at once`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Those questions matter more today than they ever have, because AI has changed both sides of marketing at once. On one side, Claude and ChatGPT can now produce in seconds what used to take a team weeks. On the other, buyers have started turning to those same engines to decide what to buy and whom to trust, which means AI is now the first reader of your brand and often the first voice to describe it. More content does not prepare a brand for that moment. Great work has never been about quantity; it has always been about quality, and no matter how powerful the models become, they cannot make a brand ready on their own. Readiness comes from knowing what is true about your company, what deserves to be communicated, and how to tell it, in the right format and through the right channel, to reach your audience.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Three of us said yes on September 18`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That conviction is why, on September 18, 2025, three of us decided to work together. Jia, Eason, and I set out to build agentic marketing solutions on that foundation, so that any brand could be ready for a world where AI stands between it and its customers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We began the way I was trained to begin, by listening. Our first months were spent on customer insights, sitting in interviews and deep dives with marketing leaders, and those conversations kept circling back to the same blind spot. Buyers had started asking AI what to buy, whom to trust, and which company leads a category, while the brands being discussed had no idea what AI was saying about them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`A year of shipping, driven by what customers asked for`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That blind spot became our first major product. In May 2026, shortly after Raejeanne Skillern, former CMO of AWS, joined us as Strategic Advisor, we launched SolCrys AEO as a governed loop that lets a brand measure its visibility, diagnose the gaps, execute the next best action, and verify what changed. We were deliberate about building a loop and not another dashboard, because a dashboard tells you where you stand while a loop helps you improve.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`MCP came next, because customers wanted to use SolCrys without leaving the environments where they already write, code, analyze, and create. We shipped it in June and watched teams turn visibility insights straight into content. Once they could see and act on how they appeared in AI answers, they wanted to know whether the answers were right, and whether being mentioned meant being chosen. In July we answered both, first with Answer Accuracy, which flags ungrounded claims and helps teams correct them, and then with Recommendation, which measures how favorably AI endorses a brand compared with its competitors. We closed the month by launching Corporate Context Engine, a living record of a company's approved facts, claims, and proof, alongside Community Intelligence, which starts with Reddit and helps teams join the conversations that shape AI answers. In August we added Signals, which monitors a customer's industry and competitors and briefs them daily so that what they publish keeps them cited. And in September we launched our web product and our animation product at AI Infra Summit, together with our design partner Cornelis.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Cornelis: three requests, one context`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Looking back, none of this came from a roadmap we dreamed up in a room. Our portfolio has been driven by customer needs, and specifically by needs we heard again and again across a broad set of customers. [Our work with Cornelis](/customers/cornelis/) shows how that happens in practice. They started working with us on AEO, then asked us to help with their webpages, and then brought us in to create the booth animation for their presence at the summit. Each request looked like a separate project at first, but every one of them drew on the same approved facts, the same claims, and the same proof.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Expanding from core AEO into webpages and animation may look like a stretch, but to us it was the natural next step. Writing copy for AI does not happen in a vacuum. The facts an AI engine cites are the same facts a buyer reads on your website and hears at your booth, and a good marketing experience meets customers wherever they are, whether those customers are AI or human, whether they are browsing the web or showing up in person.`},{type:`heading`,text:`One system, not two playbooks`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Cornelis is not an exception. Across our customers, marketing teams are finding that AI answers, webpages, community conversations, and event content can no longer be run as separate projects, because buyers and AI engines read them all as one story. What these teams need is one system: a single Corporate Context, built once, from which agents and humans work together to create every output. Today that context powers AI answers, webpages, booth animation, community engagement, and industry and competitor monitoring for the teams we work with. This is the discipline I spent nearly twenty years practicing by hand, now built into the way our customers' work gets done.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It would be tempting to handle all of this by running two playbooks, one for people and one for AI, but marketing to humans and to AI separately is not a good strategy. Your customers move between the two without noticing the seam. They read a Reddit thread, ask ChatGPT to compare options, visit your website, and stop by your booth, and they expect the same story at every stop. That is why we build everything from one context, so the story holds together whether the first reader is a person or a model.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Your brand, AI ready`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is what we mean by your brand, AI ready. You do what you are good at, building great products and serving your customers, and we make sure your brand shows up accurately and consistently wherever those customers look, whether in an AI answer, on a webpage, or at your booth.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`To Jia and Eason, thank you for saying yes on September 18. To our customers and advisors, thank you for every piece of feedback, because every feature we shipped began with something you told us. We built year one by listening, and we intend to build year two the same way.`}]},{slug:`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`,ogImage:`/og/news-introducing-the-corporate-context-engine.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Announcing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine`,metaTitle:`Announcing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine | SolCrys`,description:`Today we're introducing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine — a living record of your company that keeps AI current as your business evolves.`,date:`2026-07-28`,datePublished:`2026-07-28`,updated:`2026-07-28`,tag:`Launch`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},leadParagraph:`A month ago, we launched SolCrys Answer Accuracy to help companies catch and correct what AI says about their brands.`,relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today's buyers don't search the way they used to. They ask ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overview, Claude, Perplexity, and other AI engines, and those answers increasingly determine which vendors make the shortlist. The challenge is that AI doesn't always have the latest facts. It may describe an outdated product, repeat inaccurate third-party claims, or miss your latest positioning altogether. Even if your website is current, AI may still rely on information published months or years ago.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys Answer Accuracy helps marketing teams stay ahead of those mistakes. It continuously monitors what AI engines say about your company, flags inaccurate or unsupported claims, recommends actions to correct them, and verifies whether those corrections actually change future AI answers. For the first time, teams can measure the gap between what their company knows to be true and what AI tells their buyers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`As customers adopted Answer Accuracy, they started asking a bigger question.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Accuracy isn't something you fix once`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The problem wasn't correcting AI once—it was keeping AI current as the business evolved.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Customers launch products, update pricing, refine messaging, enter new markets, and respond to competitors. Each change affects what AI should say about their company, meaning an accurate answer today can become outdated tomorrow.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The approved information AI needs is rarely in one place. It's scattered across websites, documentation pages, launch announcements, and sales decks.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Accuracy showed customers where AI was wrong. The next question became obvious: how do you continuously keep AI aligned with your business?`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing the Corporate Context Engine`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today, we're introducing the Corporate Context Engine.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Give SolCrys a product URL, and it builds a living record of your company. Instead of relying on scattered websites, presentations, or docs, your team gets a single source of truth for your products, messaging, market, and competitors.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`As your business changes, your Corporate Context changes with it. Upload a new deck, pricing update, messaging brief, competitor announcement, or any PDF, PowerPoint, Word document, CSV, or image, and SolCrys refreshes that record automatically. There's no need to re-brief every team or start over. The latest approved context is available everywhere it is needed.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That context powers every workflow in SolCrys. Teams can understand why AI is getting their brand wrong, generate content grounded in approved messaging, and measure whether AI answers improve over time.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Several customers are already piloting the Corporate Context Engine. They're using it to keep product updates, positioning changes, and other critical company knowledge current. Some measure whether new messaging is appearing in AI-generated answers. Others track how quickly AI stops mentioning a retired product after a major website redesign. One customer uses the Engine to monitor how AI describes its products during a product recall, ensuring customers receive the latest approved information.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing Community Intelligence`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We're also launching SolCrys Community Intelligence, a new product line for the communities where buyers research, starting with Reddit.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Reddit has become one of the web's most influential sources of product research. Buyers ask questions, compare vendors, and share real-world experiences—and those conversations increasingly influence how AI answers similar questions.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Community Intelligence continuously monitors Reddit for discussions about your brand, products, competitors, and category. It identifies feature requests, misconceptions, competitive comparisons, and other conversations that matter most.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`When a discussion needs attention, SolCrys drafts a response grounded in your approved Corporate Context—your product facts, messaging, and writing guide—so your team can respond quickly and consistently. You can publish the response as generated or review and edit it before posting, giving your team full control over every interaction.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The result is a closed loop: identify the conversations shaping AI, participate with accurate information, and measure how those changes influence future AI answers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Reddit is where Community Intelligence starts, not where it stops. Over time it will extend to Hacker News, G2, TrustRadius, and the other communities that shape how buyers evaluate your category.`},{type:`heading`,text:`One foundation for every workflow`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Corporate Context Engine sits underneath the entire SolCrys platform. It captures the facts about your products, market, competitors, messaging, and writing guide, then powers Answer Accuracy, Deep Analysis, Recommended Actions, Community Intelligence, and every downstream workflow built on top of them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`This is just the beginning. Our vision is a Corporate Context Engine that becomes the foundation for every marketing workflow in SolCrys.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Accuracy tells you where AI gets your brand wrong. The Corporate Context Engine keeps the ground truth current, so AI can keep pace as your business evolves.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Ground truth, on demand.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Availability`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Corporate Context Engine and Community Intelligence are both available on the Custom plan.`},{type:`cta`,text:`See Custom plan pricing`,href:`https://app.solcrys.com/pricing`}]},{slug:`why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free`,ogImage:`/og/news-why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Why we made Prompt Pulse and the ChatGPT Visibility Tracker free`,metaTitle:`Why our AI visibility tools are free | A founder note from Eason Wang`,description:`A note from SolCrys co-founder Eason Wang on why we made two tools — the free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker and Prompt Pulse — free for any brand: seeing where you stand in AI search shouldn't be locked behind enterprise pricing.`,dek:`We made two tools free — the ChatGPT Visibility Tracker and Prompt Pulse. A note on why seeing the problem shouldn't cost what fixing it does.`,date:`2026-06-03`,datePublished:`2026-06-03`,updated:`2026-06-03`,tag:`Founder Note`,author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},leadParagraph:`AI search is becoming the front door to your brand. Buyers ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews the questions they used to type into Google, and the answer comes back naming a short list of brands. The hard part isn't improving your spot in that answer — it's that most teams have no way to even see it.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`When we sit down with marketing and brand teams, the first reaction is almost always the same: curiosity mixed with a little dread. They suspect AI engines are already shaping how buyers find them, but they can't tell whether ChatGPT recommends them, ignores them, or names a competitor. And when they go looking for the data, they hit a wall — the tools that can show them are priced for enterprises with five-figure budgets.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That bothered me. You shouldn't have to sign a five-figure contract to find out whether AI search is quietly routing your buyers to a competitor. Seeing the problem should be free. So we built two tools and gave them away.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Enter your domain, and we run the high-intent questions your buyers ask on ChatGPT — then show you where you land: mentioned, cited, or skipped, and which competitor gets named in your place. No credit card, results in about five minutes. It answers one question honestly: when someone asks ChatGPT about your category, are you in the room?`},{type:`cta`,text:`Run the free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker`,href:`/free-chatgpt-visibility-tracker/`},{type:`heading`,text:`Prompt Pulse`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The tracker tells you how you're doing on the prompts that matter. Prompt Pulse tells you which prompts matter. It's free AI-demand data: the questions buyers bring to ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Google AI Overviews, organized by industry, ranked by demand, and flagged for what's rising. We built it because "what should we even be tracking?" is the question that stalls most AEO programs before they start — and the answer was buried inside enterprise data products. Now it's a page you can read for free.`},{type:`cta`,text:`Explore Prompt Pulse`,href:`/prompt-pulse/`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why we made them free`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Here's the conviction behind both. We don't think you should pay to see the problem. You should pay — if you choose to — to fix it. The diagnosis (where do I stand, what is my market asking AI) should be open. The execution — turning those gaps into governed, on-brand content and citations your team can approve, ship, and verify — is the hard, ongoing work, and that's what SolCrys charges for.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`There's a selfish reason too, and I'd rather be honest about it. We're a young company in a category that's still being defined. The more brands that can see what's happening to them in AI search, the faster the whole category grows up — and the better the conversations we get to have. Free tools are how we earn the right to those conversations. We would rather be the team that helped you see clearly than the one that hid the mirror behind a sales call.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We also run both tools on ourselves, constantly. Like most brands new to AI search, we have our own gaps to close — and using the same instruments we hand to customers keeps us honest about what actually moves an answer.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Try them`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Run the tracker on your domain, then open Prompt Pulse for your industry. If what you see makes you want to do something about it, that's what the rest of SolCrys is for. And if it just makes the shift to AI search a little less of a black box, that's a good outcome too.`}],relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,ogImage:`/og/news-introducing-solcrys-aeo.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Introducing SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to win AI search`,metaTitle:`Introducing SolCrys AEO | A note from the founders`,description:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to measure, diagnose, fix, and verify their visibility across ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overview, Gemini, and the rest of the AI answer layer.`,dek:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO. Measure visibility. Diagnose gaps. Activate the right actions. Verify what moved. All across leading answer engines, from one workspace governed by your brand.`,date:`2026-05-20`,datePublished:`2026-05-20`,updated:`2026-05-20`,tag:`Launch`,leadParagraph:`Brand discovery is expanding beyond traditional search engines into AI search engines. Buyers now ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI Overview the questions they once typed into Google.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`For marketing teams, the impact is immediate. CMOs are building AEO strategy. SEO teams have added AEO to their scope. Campaign teams want their sponsored thought-leadership articles to surface in AI engines.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`But moving fast is hard. Teams are already using AI tools to draft content, SEO platforms to track rankings, and social listening tools to measure share of voice. None of these were built for AEO — and rolling out another tool across SEO, PMM, PR, brand, and communications can become a project of its own.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`What teams want is simpler: one place to measure their brand across every AI answer engine, find the fixes that move the needle, and ship brand-consistent content.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing SolCrys AEO`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to win AI search. Measure visibility. Diagnose gaps. Activate the right actions. Verify impact. All across leading answer engines, from one workspace governed by your brand tone and voice.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Industry-leading engine coverage`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys covers the engines that matter most today: ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overview, and Gemini. Additional engines, including shopping and retail, are available through selected plans.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Brand-safe and managed`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys is fully managed. No infrastructure to stand up and maintain. The Corporate Context Layer keeps your messaging framework, brand writing guide, and approved claims governed and secured — forming the operating boundaries for every action SolCrys recommends.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`One tool for every team and product line`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Whether you're a lean team or a multi-product enterprise, SolCrys adapts to how your organization works. For smaller organizations, SolCrys brings the full AEO workflow into one focused workspace — so teams can move faster without adding operational complexity. For larger organizations, dedicated workspaces can be created by product line or function — each grounded in its own messaging, claims, and product truth.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Seamless workflow integration`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys is built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP) — the open standard for connecting AI systems to the tools you already use. That means SolCrys plugs into your marketing stack natively: content management systems, AI writing and coding tools such as Claude Cowork, ChatGPT, and Codex. As more systems expose MCP servers, new integrations come online without custom integration work.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Product deep dive`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys AEO brings the full AEO workflow into one platform — from tracking visibility to finding gaps, shipping fixes, and proving impact.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Track the prompts that matter`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`You can't defend what you can't measure. SolCrys shows where your brand stands across leading answer engines — from mention rate and share of voice to citations and sentiment — tied to the prompts that matter most. Brand, PR, PMM, and campaign teams get one clear view your CMO can take to the board.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Find the gaps and ship the fixes`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`See which prompts your brand wins and where it's lagging. SolCrys identifies the top domains driving answers, shows where your brand is mentioned and why, then recommends actions to move the needle — from a content update or UGC strategy to a PR angle. Run content audits and manage the work in a kanban view with owners and status tracked across teams.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Verify what moved`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`After each action ships, rerun the same prompt set to measure what changed. SolCrys tracks citation rate, AI answer accuracy, recommendation share, and visibility movement, so teams can see which fixes improved the answer and which gaps still need work. Every result ties back to the original prompt, citation, and action — giving teams a clear way to prove impact.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Try it today`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The fastest way to see how AI engines are talking about your brand is to run a free audit. We'll surface where you're already winning, where you're invisible, and which fixes will move the needle first.`},{type:`cta`,text:`Start your free audit`,href:`https://app.solcrys.com/audit`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If you'd rather start with the methodology, our resource library covers AEO fundamentals, per-engine optimization playbooks, and the buyer guides we publish for marketing teams evaluating this category.`},{type:`signatures`,authors:[{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`}]}],relatedSlugs:[`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,ogImage:`/og/news-raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor.png`,kind:`press-release`,title:`SolCrys Names Raejeanne Skillern, Former AWS CMO and Intel Leader, as Strategic Advisor`,metaTitle:`SolCrys Names Raejeanne Skillern as Strategic Advisor | SolCrys News`,description:`Veteran tech executive Raejeanne Skillern, former AWS CMO and longtime Intel data center leader, joins SolCrys as a strategic advisor as the company builds the AEO operating system for brands in AI search.`,dek:`Veteran tech executive joins SolCrys to help brands get discovered and recommended in AI search.`,date:`2026-05-13`,datePublished:`2026-05-13`,updated:`2026-05-13`,tag:`Company`,heroImage:{type:`person`,name:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`,alt:`Portrait of Raejeanne Skillern, Strategic Advisor at SolCrys`},leadParagraph:`SolCrys, the AEO operating system for brands to win discovery in AI, today announced that Raejeanne Skillern — former Chief Marketing Officer at Amazon Web Services and a longtime hyperscale infrastructure leader — has joined the company as a strategic advisor.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Skillern brings more than 30 years of leadership across cloud, data center infrastructure, communications, and artificial intelligence. She has led multi-billion-dollar businesses at AWS, Intel, and Flex. Skillern currently serves on the boards of Jabil (NYSE: JBL) and Dycom Industries (NYSE: DY).`},{type:`quote`,text:`Raejeanne brings a rare combination of operating experience, market insight, and board-level perspective at a moment when AI is reshaping how companies build and grow their brands. As organizations across industries adapt to AI-driven discovery and decision-making, her experience scaling global businesses and guiding technology transformation will be invaluable to SolCrys and the customers we serve.`,attribution:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Founder and CEO, SolCrys`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`At AWS, Skillern led the global marketing organization, helping to modernize marketing for AI. At Flex, she ran a multi-billion P&L as President of the Communications, Enterprise & Cloud Division, repositioning the business for cloud, optical, and 5G. Over 25 years at Intel, she built the hyperscale data center business alongside the world's leading cloud providers.`},{type:`quote`,text:`AI answer engines are changing how customers discover, evaluate, and choose companies, but most organizations are not yet equipped to manage their presence in this new environment. SolCrys gives companies a clear way to measure how they appear across AI platforms, identify what needs improvement, strengthen their visibility, and track results — while staying true to their brand. SolCrys is building the category-defining platform for how companies manage brand visibility and trust in the AI era.`,attribution:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`},{type:`heading`,text:`About SolCrys`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys was founded by a team of AI, data, search, marketing, and growth veterans from Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Alibaba, and Tencent, building the AEO operating system for the next era of brand discovery. The company today serves brands across HPC, AI, consumer goods, retail, and beyond.`}],relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`,ogImage:`/og/news-welcoming-raejeanne-skillern.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Welcoming Raejeanne Skillern to SolCrys`,metaTitle:`Welcoming Raejeanne Skillern to SolCrys | Founder Note`,description:`A note from SolCrys CEO Gwen Chen on why Raejeanne Skillern — former AWS CMO and longtime Intel hyperscale leader — is joining SolCrys as a strategic advisor, and what it signals about where the company is headed.`,dek:`A note from our founder on why Raejeanne is joining — and what it signals about where the AEO category, and SolCrys, are headed.`,date:`2026-05-13`,datePublished:`2026-05-13`,updated:`2026-05-13`,tag:`Founder Note`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},heroImage:{type:`person`,name:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`,alt:`Raejeanne Skillern, newly joined SolCrys strategic advisor`},leadParagraph:`When we started SolCrys, we made a bet that AI answer engines would fundamentally change how customers discover, evaluate, and choose brands — and that most marketing teams weren't equipped for that shift yet.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Nine months in, that bet is no longer speculative. Buyers are starting product research in ChatGPT instead of Google. Shoppers are getting recommendations from Amazon Alexa for Shopping and Walmart Sparky before they see a listing. Google AI Mode is changing search from a list of links into AI-generated answers that increasingly shape how customers discover brands. The companies winning are the ones treating AI visibility as something they can measure, diagnose, fix, and prove — not a black box.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is the operating system we are building at SolCrys. And today, I'm thrilled to share that Raejeanne Skillern is joining us as a strategic advisor.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why Raejeanne, and why now`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Raejeanne spent more than 30 years inside the buildout of modern cloud infrastructure — at Intel, where she built the hyperscale data center business alongside AWS, Azure, and Google; at Flex, where she ran a multi-billion-dollar P&L for cloud, optical, and 5G; and most recently as CMO at AWS, where she led marketing through the early phase of the generative AI shift.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`She has seen, more than almost anyone in the industry, how an inflection point in infrastructure rewrites how every category gets sold. That perspective is exactly what we need right now, for three main reasons.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`1. She understands what changes when a category moves from search-led to recommendation-led discovery.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The buyer journey for cloud compute did not simply change — the entire vendor evaluation framework changed. The same shift is now happening for consumer brands, B2B software, retail SKUs, and professional services inside the AI answer layer. Most of our customers are still operating with SEO-era playbooks. Raejeanne has lived through this kind of transition before, and she can help us help them.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`2. She has scaled marketing organizations that operate as one team alongside product and sales.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Engine Optimization is not just an SEO-team problem. It requires collaboration across SEO, PMM, brand, communications, PR, product, and sales. SolCrys is built to be the connective tissue between these teams, and Raejeanne has built the operating models that make this kind of cross-functional execution work at scale.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`3. She brings board-level perspective at exactly the right time for SolCrys.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Raejeanne sits on the boards of Jabil (NYSE: JBL) and Dycom Industries (NYSE: DY). The questions boards are now asking about AI exposure — what is our visibility, where are we losing share, is this measured — are exactly the questions SolCrys is built to answer. Having someone on the team who thinks about technology adoption from both sides of the boardroom table sharpens how we build, what we prioritize, and how we define the category.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What this signals for the brands we serve`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If you're a customer, advisor, partner, or prospect reading this: Raejeanne's involvement is a signal of where we're investing. Over the next two quarters we are doubling down on three things.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`Cross-engine measurement depth — moving beyond mention and citation counting to true Share of Recommendation and answer-accuracy tracking, prompt-by-prompt, across ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overviews / AI Mode, Perplexity, Amazon Alexa for Shopping, Walmart Sparky, and ChatGPT Shopping.`,`Corporate Context as the brand operating layer — ensuring every action SolCrys recommends is grounded in your approved stats, claims, and guardrails by default, so your content stays on-brand, accurate, and defensible.`,`The execution loop — closing the gap between 'we found the gap' and 'we fixed it', with draft briefs to media outlets, analysts and influencers, FAQ updates, and content actions tied to measurable movements in AI answers.`]},{type:`heading`,text:`Closing`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`I started SolCrys with my co-founders Eason and Jia because we believed brand discovery was about to be rewritten, and the teams building inside that change deserved a real operating system, not another dashboard.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Bringing Raejeanne onto the team will help us better serve our customers as they navigate this next phase of AI discovery.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Welcome, Raejeanne. We're thrilled to have you with us.`}],relatedSlugs:[`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,`introducing-solcrys-aeo`]}]};export{e as t};